        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe change is well-shaped overall: `FlagOwnerBulkSignup` faithfully mirrors its sibling `CountOwnerNamesakes` (same `service(@Val Owner, OwnerRepository)` form, same \"frozen at creation\" javadoc, same pipeline placement before `SaveOwner`), the entity field/getter/setter parallel `namesakeCount`, and the schema/OpenAPI updates are complete and consistent. One structural issue:\n\n**Duplicated per-day owner count \u2014 extract a shared helper.**\n`FlagOwnerBulkSignup.service` computes the day's signups with code that is verbatim identical to `CheckOwnerDailyLimit.service`:\n\n```java\nLocalDate businessDay = BusinessDays.effectiveRegistrationDate(...);\nlong createdToday = ownerRepository.findAll().stream()\n        .filter(existing -> businessDay.equals(existing.getRegistrationDate()))\n        .count();\n```\n\nUnlike the other `findAll` loops in this package (namesakes counts by *name* \u2014 a different computation), these two functions count the exact same thing: owners already registered on a given business day. They only differ in threshold (80 vs 100) and outcome (flag vs reject). The new javadoc even calls this out \u2014 *\"The same per-day accumulation the daily-limit rule uses, at a lower, non-rejecting threshold\"* \u2014 which signals a shared concept implemented twice. This is precisely the drift risk the codebase explicitly guards against: `BusinessDays.effectiveRegistrationDate` exists as a \"single source of truth shared by every step \u2026 so they cannot drift apart,\" yet the count built on top of it is now copy-pasted.\n\nSuggested fix: add one shared counting method (e.g. a `static long createdOn(OwnerRepository, LocalDate businessDay)` alongside `BusinessDays`, or a small `OwnerDailyCounts` helper) and have both `CheckOwnerDailyLimit` and `FlagOwnerBulkSignup` call it. Each function then keeps only its own threshold and action, and the two rules stay aligned by construction."
      }
